`lib/App/karr/BoardStore.pm:176-181`. `allocate_next_id` reads the next-id ref,
increments, and writes it back with no compare-and-swap. Two agents that read
the same value both get it.

Module level, 8 forked processes with a wall-clock barrier: 8 calls returned
only **2 distinct ids** — six of them got id 1.

Through the real CLI, 5 rounds of 8 parallel `karr create`, every one exit 0:

    round 1: created=8  on board=6
    round 2: created=16 on board=12
    round 5: created=40 on board=32

**40 successful creates produced 32 tasks.** The collision is visible in the
output itself:

    Created task 36: final-8
    Created task 36: final-1
    Created task 38: final-2
    Created task 38: final-3

Each duplicate pair writes the same `refs/karr/tasks/N/data` and the loser is
destroyed. Every process reports success; nothing is logged.

This matters because karr-foundation runs parallel agents against one board,
which is the documented use case. Fix needs a compare-and-swap on the next-id
ref (write only if the ref still has the value we read, retry on mismatch) —
libgit2's reference update takes an expected-old-value for exactly this.
Unblocked: the upstream CAS API is released and pinned (#80, #83, commit 3d54e03).

allocate_next_id can now do a real compare-and-swap instead of
reference_create(force => 1): read the current next-id ref, then write with
Git::Native::Repository::reference_set_target($name, $new, expected_old =>
$old). A concurrent writer makes that throw a Git::Native::Error with
is_not_matched true — catch it, re-read, retry in a bounded loop rather than
letting the last writer win. For the first ever write use
reference_create(..., expected_old => undef), which atomically requires the ref
to be absent.

cpanfile is on Git::Native 0.004 / Git::Libgit2 0.005, so nothing external is
missing any more.
Reproduced 2026-08-09 before the fix: 12 parallel `karr create` on a fresh
board produced 11 tasks, ids #1..#11. One create silently overwrote another's
id allocation.

Probe result on the retry contract (see #85): under contention the failure is
usually GIT_ELOCKED (-14), not is_not_matched — libgit2 holds a refs/*.lock for
the write. A loop that retries only is_not_matched still loses writes. Retry on
both, with randomised backoff.
Fixed (working tree, not committed -- maintainer reviews the diff).

allocate_next_id is now a compare-and-swap on refs/karr/meta/next-id, with the
counter re-read INSIDE each attempt. New in App::karr::Git: read_ref_with_oid
(OID + content from one read), write_ref_cas (1 landed / 0 lost the race /
dies on a real error; undef expected_old = must-not-exist), retry_contended
(the one place the retry policy lives), allocate_next_id_ref.
BoardStore::allocate_next_id delegates; public API unchanged.

The trap: under real contention the common libgit2 failure is NOT
GIT_EMODIFIED but GIT_ELOCKED (-14) -- libgit2 takes a refs/<name>.lock file.
Git::Native::Error has no predicate for it, so it is compared against the code
imported from Git::Libgit2. A loop retrying only is_not_matched still loses.

Verified end to end: 12 parallel 'karr create' on a fresh board -> 12 tasks,
ids 1..12 (was 11 tasks with a duplicated id).

Regression test t/75-ref-cas-contention.t (12 forked contenders on a
wall-clock barrier). Against the old code it fails: allocations came back as
{1 x6, 2 x2, 3 x2} and 2 processes died on the raw libgit2 error.

Full suite: prove -lr t/ -> Files=73, Tests=460, Result: PASS.
Fixed in 4f4b375. allocate_next_id now goes through
App::karr::Git::allocate_next_id_ref: read the counter and its OID together,
write guarded by that OID, re-read inside each attempt. Verified: 12 parallel
`karr create` produce 12 tasks with ids 1..12 (was 11 tasks). Regression test
t/75-ref-cas-contention.t, which fails against the pre-fix code.
